+<section id="desktop-receivers" class="doc-section"><h2>Windows and Linux receiver previews</h2>
+<p>Version 0.1.0 is an unsigned preview. Native controller input, disconnect release, installation and pairing callbacks pass automated Windows and Linux checks. Real WoW, Steam and Proton acceptance is still pending. Keep your keyboard and mouse available and verify your controls in a quiet place.</p>
+<p>You need the Thumble phone app, WoW, a compatible ConsolePort release and both devices on the same Wi-Fi. Public iPhone and Android apps are coming soon. The preview receiver uses the same pairing protocol as the Mac receiver; older phone builds call the scanner <strong>Connect Mac</strong>.</p>
+<h3>Windows 10 or 11, x64</h3><ol><li><a href="/downloads/receiver-latest-windows.exe">Download the Windows installer</a>. This preview is not code signed, so Windows may show an unknown-publisher warning.</li><li>Install the <a href="https://github.com/nefarius/ViGEmBus/releases/tag/v1.22.0">official ViGEmBus driver</a> separately and reboot if requested. ViGEmBus is archived and is the preview’s compatibility backend. Thumble setup does not install it.</li><li>Run the receiver installer for your user, then open <strong>Thumble Receiver</strong> from Start. Allow inbound traffic for the receiver on your private network if Windows Firewall asks. Windows Server and ARM PCs are not supported targets.</li></ol>
+<h3>Linux, x64</h3><p>Extract the archive, then install for your user:</p><pre><code>tar -xzf Thumble-Receiver-0.1.0-linux-x64.tar.gz
+./thumble-receiver/thumble-receiver --install-user</code></pre>
+<p>The installer requires <code>xdg-utils</code>. Open <strong>Thumble Receiver</strong> from your applications menu. Keep the bundle’s <code>_internal</code> folder alongside its executable.</p>
+<p>Your user needs access to <code>/dev/uinput</code>. On a desktop using systemd-logind, an administrator can configure:</p><pre><code>sudo modprobe uinput
+echo 'KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"' | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/70-thumble-uinput.rules
+sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
+sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=misc --sysname-match=uinput</code></pre>
+<p>Log out and back in if permissions do not update. Run Thumble as your normal user. Distribution and session policies may need administrator configuration. Do not make uinput world writable. Linux chat additionally requires <code>xdotool</code> and X11 or XWayland; pure Wayland chat is unavailable.</p>
+<h3>Pair and verify your controls</h3><ol><li>Open the receiver and scan its QR from Thumble on the same Wi-Fi. If the selected address belongs to a VPN, choose your Wi-Fi address and select <strong>Restart here</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>Start demo / Resume</strong> on the phone. Follow ConsolePort’s setup, then test movement and stopping, camera, an action, a modifier and menus.</li><li>For label sync, choose the WoW installation folder in the receiver. On Linux, select the game folder inside the Wine or Proton prefix. Enable ThumbleBridge sync and use <code>/reload</code> in WoW.</li><li>Pause or disconnect before leaving. Pair again after receiver restart or system sleep. Verify a stop after disconnect before playing.</li></ol>
+<p>The free receiver uses the phone’s 30-minute total demo or Pro entitlement. Closing the receiver window keeps it in the tray when available; choose Quit to exit. Windows uninstalls through Apps settings. Linux uninstalls with <code>~/.local/bin/thumble-receiver --uninstall-user</code>. Both retain profiles and purchase/demo records.</p>
+<p>Compare the SHA-256 checksum before installing: <code>Get-FileHash .\Thumble-Receiver-0.1.0-windows-x64-setup.exe -Algorithm SHA256</code> on Windows, or <code>sha256sum Thumble-Receiver-0.1.0-linux-x64.tar.gz</code> on Linux. <a href="/downloads/receiver-desktop-setup.txt">Full receiver instructions</a> · <a href="/downloads/Thumble-Receiver-0.1.0-desktop.json">Release hashes and validation</a> · <a href="/#download">All receiver downloads</a>.</p></section>
+<section id="game-layouts" class="doc-section"><span id="receivers"></span><h2>Our initial focus</h2><p>We’re building first for WoW on Mac with ConsolePort. Compatibility depends on your WoW client and addon version; complete the in-game checks for your setup. Windows and Linux receiver previews are now available. Read the <a href="#desktop-receivers">preview setup instructions</a> and complete the in-game checks for your setup.</p></section>
 <section id="agents" class="doc-section"><span id="keypad-editor"></span><span id="appearance"></span><span id="cli"></span><h2>About earlier AI layout guides</h2><p>For authenticated read-only agent integrations, see the <a href="/developers">Thumble developer documentation, API and CLI</a>.</p><p>AI/MCP controller generation is currently disabled while we focus on the built-in ConsolePort experience. No AI agent or cloud account is needed to pair and play. Existing saved controllers are retained. Older blog posts describe earlier development versions.</p></section>
 <section id="safety" class="doc-section"><h2>Taking a break</h2><p>Pause or disconnect when you stop playing. The receiver releases held controls on disconnect, Mac sleep or demo expiry. Return through the Thumble menu when you’re ready.</p></section>
 <section id="faq" class="doc-section"><h2>Get ready for launch</h2><p><a href="/waitlist">Join the launch list</a> for phone app news. The <a href="/#download">Mac receiver</a> is available now; you’ll need the phone app to use it.</p></section>
